WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.090
Hello, and welcome back to another
video with ItsDrDan, and today

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:13.270
we're going to be learning how to
find percent composition by mass.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.020
Which, the whole entire idea behind
this is, this is something that

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:22.430
allows chemists to find the amount of
an element within a given compound.

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:26.209
So it allows us to be able to figure
out essentially how pure a substance is.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.610
Now the way that we're going to
look at this with percents is just

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:31.640
like any other mathematical steps.

00:00:32.049 --> 00:00:36.929
is that a percent is the part over the
whole, or more specifically, we're going

00:00:36.929 --> 00:00:40.979
to be assuming that we have a hundred gram
sample and trying to figure out, well,

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:43.130
how much of it is within that sample.

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:45.010
So let's take a whole look at this.

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:47.370
So what we're going to be
using is the following formula.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:52.220
So what this is, is the mass percent
is the total of a given element.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:53.950
So how much of that mass there is.

00:00:54.275 --> 00:00:58.584
using the molar mass and then
dividing that by the molar mass

00:00:58.644 --> 00:01:01.204
of the compound times 100%.

00:01:01.574 --> 00:01:03.815
So this is a formula that we're going
to be using throughout this entire

00:01:03.824 --> 00:01:08.115
video, going through multiple different
examples of how you can calculate

00:01:08.445 --> 00:01:12.154
percent composition and how you
can figure out how much of a sample

00:01:12.154 --> 00:01:14.115
you have with a given mass as well.

00:01:14.115 --> 00:01:16.475
So we're going to go through
several different examples.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.910
And let's try to take a look
at three easy steps for how we

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:22.090
can do these types of problems.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.640
So with empirical formulas, the
great thing about it is that there's

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:38.390
three simple steps for how to do
every single one of these problems.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:42.529
The first thing you're going to do
is find the total molar mass of each

00:01:42.579 --> 00:01:44.649
element within the given compound.

00:01:45.199 --> 00:01:48.949
meaning that we have to count
the number of atoms of each type.

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:52.990
So if there's two carbons, we got
to make sure we multiply our molar

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:55.040
mass of carbon by two, for example.

00:01:55.609 --> 00:01:59.020
Our second step is we need to
find the molar mass of the entire,

00:01:59.049 --> 00:02:01.269
the entire compound as a whole.

00:02:01.789 --> 00:02:04.979
Um, being that that is going to
be the denominator of our formula.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:09.910
Last step is you just plug it
all in into your formula and that

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:12.929
is what we're going to be doing
in all these different examples.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:16.549
Let's go into an example that we can
do together and as you're going through

00:02:16.549 --> 00:02:19.549
these, please feel free to try to
pause it and try to do them on your

00:02:19.549 --> 00:02:21.119
own to see if you can do them as well.

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:23.570
Okay, let's do the following problem.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:27.350
So it says, calculate the
percent composition by mass

00:02:27.815 --> 00:02:33.355
of C and O in CO2, which more
specifically is carbon and oxygen.

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:34.910
So let's go through it.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.990
Well, our first step was for us to be
able to calculate the amount of molar

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:42.970
mass for each element within the compound.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:46.170
So the first thing you're going
to have to do is look up the molar

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:50.250
masses from the periodic table,
which I did for us for this problem.

00:02:50.829 --> 00:02:56.880
So if we have our compound, which in this
case is CO2, What we have to do is count

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:02.630
the number of each atom to calculate
what the total Mass is for each element.

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:09.170
So for example in CO2 I have one
carbon atom So what I'm going to do is

00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:11.449
multiply that one by a smaller mass.

00:03:11.459 --> 00:03:13.815
So if I do one times 12.

00:03:13.895 --> 00:03:18.545
01 grams per mole, I'm going to get 12.

00:03:18.595 --> 00:03:19.985
01 grams per mole.

00:03:20.955 --> 00:03:25.944
Now, for oxygen, right, what are we
going to do for the other portion?

00:03:25.945 --> 00:03:29.315
Well, with oxygen, we need
to count both atoms, right?

00:03:29.315 --> 00:03:31.135
We have two atoms of oxygen.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:34.750
So we'll take 2 times 15.

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:41.850
99 grams per mole and that's going
to be, that's going to equal 31.

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:43.480
98 grams per mole.

00:03:44.239 --> 00:03:45.930
So that is for the first part.

00:03:46.319 --> 00:03:49.680
Now for the second step, what we're
going to do is we're going to take those

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:56.150
values Um, and add them up to find what
the total molar mass is of our compound.

00:03:56.799 --> 00:04:01.549
So if we add up both, both
these values here, we're going

00:04:01.549 --> 00:04:04.030
to get up our molecular mass.

00:04:04.039 --> 00:04:12.510
So our molar mass for the compound, the
molar mass of CO2 is going to be 12.

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:12.604
01.

00:04:12.905 --> 00:04:14.245
Plus 31.

00:04:14.295 --> 00:04:20.335
98 grams per mole, and what
we will get in the end is 43.

00:04:20.425 --> 00:04:23.895
99 grams per mole, or CO2.

00:04:24.745 --> 00:04:27.825
Now our third step is just
taking all those values and

00:04:27.835 --> 00:04:29.565
plugging them into our formula.

00:04:29.995 --> 00:04:33.435
So let's first do for the
first one, which was carbon.

00:04:33.965 --> 00:04:40.235
Okay, so with carbon, we had a value of,
uh, So let's write down our elements.

00:04:40.235 --> 00:04:42.745
We have carbon, and we have oxygen.

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:45.610
So carbon had 12.

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:52.520
01 grams per mole in CO2,
and all that was over 43.

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:54.240
99 grams per mole.

00:04:57.659 --> 00:05:01.560
Now what we will do is we're going
to multiply this by 100 percent

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:05.729
in order to get our percentage
of carbon within our sample.

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:10.950
So when we do our math here,
what we are going to get is 27.

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:11.650
32%.

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:14.390
of carbon.

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:18.340
Now for oxygen, we're going to
be doing the same general thing.

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:20.970
And keep in mind all of these
different elements that you

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:23.050
have, the total should equal 100.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:25.739
So there's always a way for
you to double check as well.

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:30.150
So when we do the math
here, we should get 31.

00:05:30.189 --> 00:05:32.559
98 grams per mole, right?

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:33.879
Which was the part of carbon.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:38.010
over the whole, which is the 43.

00:05:38.049 --> 00:05:43.859
99 grams per mole, what we got
in part two, times 100, and

00:05:43.859 --> 00:05:46.739
what we are going to get is 72.

00:05:47.289 --> 00:05:48.189
68%.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:51.880
for our final answer.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:54.020
So when we see CO2.

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:59.300
32 percent makes up carbon and 72.

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:01.260
68 percent makes up oxygen.

00:06:01.879 --> 00:06:05.130
Let's try to do another
example just like this one.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:09.629
For the second example, what we have
is ammonium nitrate, which is NH4NO3.

00:06:11.895 --> 00:06:15.635
And this is used as a fertilizer
to add nitrogen to soil.

00:06:16.175 --> 00:06:20.005
And what it asks you to do is
calculate the percent composition by

00:06:20.005 --> 00:06:24.075
mass of N, H, and O of the compound.

00:06:24.555 --> 00:06:26.185
So, exactly what do we need to do?

00:06:26.185 --> 00:06:29.934
Well, if you remember, our very
first step is to find the total

00:06:29.944 --> 00:06:32.635
molecular, molar mass of each element.

00:06:33.225 --> 00:06:37.645
So, here I have on our, I have our little
handy dandy periodic table, um, just

00:06:37.645 --> 00:06:39.375
with each element already found for us.

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:43.710
So we need to count every single
element that exists in a species.

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:45.970
Let's first start with nitrogen.

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:51.720
So nitrogen, when we look at the formula
for this particular element, we see that

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:55.070
we have two nitrogens in the compound.

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:56.869
There's one, and then there's two.

00:06:57.410 --> 00:06:59.909
So with these kind of compounds,
we need to make sure we count

00:07:00.049 --> 00:07:03.309
every single element in order to
make sure we count it correctly.

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:09.430
So if there are two of them, what we
are going to do is take two times, its

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:16.430
molecular mass, which is 4.01 grams
per mole, and that that total is gonna

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:21.680
be 28.02 grams per mole for nitrogen.

00:07:22.829 --> 00:07:26.079
Now, we gotta do the same thing
for oxygen, so being that we have

00:07:26.239 --> 00:07:31.459
3 atoms of oxygen in this formula,
so we have oxygen, and we have 3 of

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:33.930
them, that's gonna be 3 times 15.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:39.619
99 grams per mole, and
that's going to equal 47.

00:07:39.739 --> 00:07:42.250
97 grams per mole.

00:07:43.179 --> 00:07:46.379
Now when we go through this again,
right, we can, we now count how many

00:07:46.379 --> 00:07:49.239
hydrogens there are this time, and
we can see that there's a total of.

00:07:49.295 --> 00:07:53.025
4 hydrogens that are within this
formula, so we'll have to take

00:07:53.025 --> 00:07:56.505
that number 4, times it by 1.

00:07:56.575 --> 00:07:56.914
008.

00:07:57.664 --> 00:08:02.284
So, now that will give us the
masses for each individual part,

00:08:02.325 --> 00:08:07.084
which will help us feed into how
much of each percent do we have.

00:08:07.535 --> 00:08:12.144
So it'd be 4 times 1.008, which is 4.

00:08:12.145 --> 00:08:17.055
032 total grams per mole for this species.

00:08:17.104 --> 00:08:20.565
Now the good thing is about this
is by counting all these elements,

00:08:20.955 --> 00:08:24.844
this allows us to figure out exactly
what the total molar mass is of the

00:08:24.844 --> 00:08:28.775
entire element, because what we're
going to do is we're going to take

00:08:28.835 --> 00:08:32.494
all these values that we have here
and we're going to add them all up.

00:08:32.905 --> 00:08:35.135
So we'll take that 28.

00:08:35.135 --> 00:08:37.564
02 plus the 47.

00:08:37.565 --> 00:08:37.584
97.

00:08:37.955 --> 00:08:38.385
Plus the 4.

00:08:38.385 --> 00:08:44.695
032, and what we're going to get
is the total molar mass here, okay?

00:08:44.695 --> 00:08:50.825
So when, now when we do that,
we'll get a total mass of 80.02

00:08:51.195 --> 00:08:54.635
grams per mole, or NH4NO3.

00:08:56.745 --> 00:08:59.055
Okay, so that is the total molar mass.

00:09:00.574 --> 00:09:01.665
What is step three?

00:09:01.715 --> 00:09:04.975
Well, step three is taking
these individual values and

00:09:04.975 --> 00:09:07.145
plugging them into our formula.

00:09:07.655 --> 00:09:09.615
So let's do that for ourselves.

00:09:09.615 --> 00:09:12.655
So let's blow up that formula so we can
make it a little bit bigger so we can

00:09:12.655 --> 00:09:14.035
remember exactly what we have to do.

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:18.040
So, we need to take the total mass
of each individual element, so

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:19.980
let's first start with nitrogen.

00:09:20.959 --> 00:09:22.880
So nitrogen had 28.

00:09:22.930 --> 00:09:27.719
02 as its value, so 28.

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:35.470
02 grams per mole, and let's move
that over just a little bit, and what

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:40.680
we are going to do is we're going to
divide that by the total, which was 80.

00:09:40.790 --> 00:09:42.090
02,

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:49.130
and the whole thing is going
to be multiplied by 100 in

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:51.160
order to make it as a percent.

00:09:51.694 --> 00:09:55.585
Now when we go through
that, we should get 35.

00:09:55.655 --> 00:09:58.605
02 percent for nitrogen.

00:09:59.685 --> 00:10:03.015
Now for oxygen, we're going to be
doing the exact same steps, right?

00:10:03.015 --> 00:10:07.894
We take our partial, our
partial mass, which is the 47.

00:10:07.895 --> 00:10:10.035
97 we see up there in red
all the way at the top.

00:10:10.715 --> 00:10:12.975
So we'll take that 47.

00:10:13.045 --> 00:10:20.265
97 grams per mole, and we're
going to divide that by our 80.

00:10:20.335 --> 00:10:22.665
02 grams per mole.

00:10:23.839 --> 00:10:28.180
And we'll times that by 100%, and
I'll put that on the left there just

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:29.319
to kind of show the whole thing.

00:10:29.930 --> 00:10:32.360
And what we're going to see is
oxygen is the biggest portion

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:34.000
of this, and we will get 59.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:38.359
95%, okay, for that.

00:10:39.015 --> 00:10:43.255
Now let's also shove in hydrogen in here
somewhere, so hydrogen we'll do as the

00:10:43.255 --> 00:10:45.844
last step, and that has a part of 4.

00:10:45.845 --> 00:10:52.334
032 grams per mole, which is all the
way on the top right of your screen, and

00:10:52.334 --> 00:10:55.675
we are going to divide that by that 80.

00:10:55.675 --> 00:10:58.509
02 grams per mole, times 100.

00:10:59.799 --> 00:11:08.240
Okay, now, when we get that last
percent, what we're going to get is 5.

00:11:08.689 --> 00:11:09.280
03%.

00:11:09.809 --> 00:11:12.709
Now to kind of make sure that when
you, before you turn in any of your

00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:17.349
work to a teacher for assignment, you
kind of highlight or box them to show

00:11:17.409 --> 00:11:18.970
every single one of those percent.

00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:21.050
So we use the same
formula for each percent.

00:11:21.850 --> 00:11:25.690
Let's go on to our next problem
to show another example of this,

00:11:25.690 --> 00:11:26.800
and that'll be our last one.

00:11:27.439 --> 00:11:31.189
Okay, so the following problem,
in our last example, it says The

00:11:31.189 --> 00:11:34.610
formula for rust is F E 2 0 3.

00:11:35.499 --> 00:11:40.629
How many grams of iron
are in 22.8 grams of rust?

00:11:41.180 --> 00:11:45.409
So this one's a little bit of a little
bit of a stoichiometry problem, but this

00:11:45.409 --> 00:11:49.490
is something that will help you figure
out how to use empirical formulas.

00:11:50.005 --> 00:11:55.085
So we only need to find how much the mass
percent is of iron in this case, right?

00:11:55.545 --> 00:11:57.865
So what is that mass percent?

00:11:58.454 --> 00:12:01.684
So let's go through it with our
first step, right, which is find the

00:12:01.685 --> 00:12:06.065
total molar mass of each element,
which we have the molar masses here.

00:12:06.415 --> 00:12:11.365
We only are interested in iron, but we
still need to do both pieces to find what

00:12:11.365 --> 00:12:13.885
the total molar masses, which is step two.

00:12:15.255 --> 00:12:19.395
Our very first part is being that we
have two iron molecules, so we have

00:12:19.395 --> 00:12:21.104
two of them 'cause of our subscript.

00:12:21.344 --> 00:12:24.005
I mentioned that means that we have Fe2O3.

00:12:24.795 --> 00:12:29.865
We're gonna take that two
times 55.85 grams per mole.

00:12:31.285 --> 00:12:35.834
And we are going to get, um,
when we do that math, it's 111.

00:12:35.994 --> 00:12:39.114
7 grams per mole of iron.

00:12:39.905 --> 00:12:45.144
Now the second part is oxygen, and we
have a subscript of 3 in this compound.

00:12:45.704 --> 00:12:47.545
So we have 3 times 15.

00:12:47.714 --> 00:12:55.084
99 grams per mole, and we are
going to get a value of 47.

00:12:55.155 --> 00:13:00.405
97 grams per mole for
our answer for that part.

00:13:01.194 --> 00:13:05.905
Okay, now step two, we got to find the
molar mass of the whole entire compound,

00:13:06.515 --> 00:13:11.354
meaning that we have to add up both
of the values that we just calculated.

00:13:11.354 --> 00:13:13.494
So we got to add up the 111.

00:13:13.495 --> 00:13:16.554
7 plus 47.97.

00:13:16.574 --> 00:13:17.695
So let's rewrite that.

00:13:17.695 --> 00:13:18.804
So it's 111.

00:13:19.255 --> 00:13:25.474
7 grams per mole plus 47.

00:13:25.475 --> 00:13:26.305
97 grams per mole.

00:13:27.255 --> 00:13:30.855
Now, when we do that math, what we
are going to end up getting is 159.

00:13:32.214 --> 00:13:40.675
67 grams per mole of
Fe2O3 of our species here.

00:13:41.275 --> 00:13:42.865
So that is your total molar mass.

00:13:43.535 --> 00:13:46.275
Now the last step is to find the percents.

00:13:46.705 --> 00:13:48.715
Now, which two values do we need?

00:13:48.725 --> 00:13:52.155
Well, we are interested in
iron in this particular one.

00:13:52.585 --> 00:13:57.095
So when we go through our last and final
step here in order to find the portion

00:13:57.095 --> 00:14:02.294
of iron, let that value before was 111.

00:14:02.295 --> 00:14:02.844
7.

00:14:02.845 --> 00:14:05.265
So we'll take that 111.

00:14:05.785 --> 00:14:09.465
7 grams per mole of
iron, which is our part.

00:14:10.225 --> 00:14:13.895
And we're going to divide that by the 159.

00:14:13.985 --> 00:14:21.945
67 grams per mole of Fe2O3,
times that by 100 percent.

00:14:22.690 --> 00:14:25.370
And what we are going to get is 69.

00:14:25.430 --> 00:14:31.010
96 percent of, of iron in this sample.

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:34.960
How does that apply to a mass sample?

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:39.090
Well what that allows you to do, this
is a conversion term that we can use

00:14:39.090 --> 00:14:42.830
to be able to figure out, okay, how
much iron is there in a given sample?

00:14:42.839 --> 00:14:43.590
So let's take a look.

00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:45.060
What was the original value?

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:47.260
So we were given that we had 22.

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:49.050
8 grams of rust.

00:14:49.340 --> 00:14:50.910
that we collected in our sample.

00:14:51.369 --> 00:14:56.010
So let's go back down here and try
to see exactly how can we use that.

00:14:56.469 --> 00:14:58.030
So we have 22.

00:14:58.130 --> 00:14:59.949
8 grams of Fe2O3.

00:15:02.419 --> 00:15:07.460
Now a percent is the same thing as
saying out of 100, right, for every 100.

00:15:08.764 --> 00:15:12.875
So the way that we can use
this thing to our advantage is

00:15:12.875 --> 00:15:15.724
saying that, okay, I have 69.

00:15:15.795 --> 00:15:27.744
96 grams of iron all over 100 grams
of Fe2O3, right, per cent out of 100.

00:15:28.245 --> 00:15:31.185
So this is a conversion
term that you can use.

00:15:31.569 --> 00:15:32.750
to cancel out grams.

00:15:32.780 --> 00:15:36.790
Now you can also simply just
multiply the sample by the decimal

00:15:36.790 --> 00:15:39.140
value too, which is that 0.

00:15:39.250 --> 00:15:41.249
6996, you'd get the same answer.

00:15:41.770 --> 00:15:44.859
But it's good to be able to see
how this works as a dimensional

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:48.069
analysis or a stoichiometry problem,
being that you always have to

00:15:48.069 --> 00:15:49.759
show your work in all classes.

00:15:50.420 --> 00:15:54.510
So when you do this right, you multiply
what's on top, and then divide what

00:15:54.520 --> 00:15:57.030
is on the bottom, and you will get 15.

